          "question": "Does exercise help knee osteoarthritis or make it worse?",
          "answer": "Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n\nThe retrieved material suggests exercise is a core part of managing knee osteoarthritis, but it does not directly answer whether exercise can make it worse.\n\nAccording to the document \"Exercising with osteoarthritis: what the program contains and how it feels,\" an exercise program for knee osteoarthritis combines three parts. Strengthening targets the muscles around the knee, such as the quadriceps and hip muscles. Range-of-movement work keeps the joint moving. Aerobic activity supports general fitness and body weight. The same document says that starting under supervision matters, because the right movements, enough load, and gradual progression are hard to judge alone.\n\nThe results did not cover whether any type of exercise could aggravate knee pain or damage, how much benefit to expect, or which activities to avoid. The document also refers to a separate osteoarthritis guideline note that the search did not return.\n\nIf you are considering exercise for your knee, a doctor, physiotherapist, or other clinician who knows your history can tell you which activities suit you and how to start safely.",
